Same grid, box, harness and byte-identity precondition as
`perf_gap_2026-08-11.meta`; all 24 cells byte-identical to C in both.

Four byte-identical commits, all of them REMOVING A DUPLICATED COPY of
something the encoder had already computed — none of them making an
allocation cheaper:

| commit | what | A/B at p10 |
|---|---|---|
| `29847e5d3` | the frame's block-decision set was materialised FOUR times (one of the four written and never read) | 1.072-1.111x |
| `6ad044d00` | `to_choice` cloned seven of the winner's buffers only because it ran before `commit_leaf` | 1.016-1.027x |
| `81a1bb111` | depth-0 qcoeff "unpack" that was a byte-for-byte copy on every non-64-dim block; DPB `refresh` cloning the whole picture 8x per KEY frame | 1.012-1.022x |
| this one | the per-SB recon staging buffer (alloc + zero-fill + second pass over every pixel) | null, see below |

The per-SB staging removal measures **null** (0.9972-1.0077 at n=25 against a
control reading 1.0012-1.0136) and is kept only because it is strictly less
work — one fewer allocation, one fewer zero-fill and one fewer pass per
superblock — not because it was measured to help.

**p2 has not moved all session** (4.12 -> 4.16 -> 4.14, inside its own spread:
its 1024 cell is a single ~2 s encode and the fit leans on it). Every change
here is a per-coded-block cost, and p2 spends roughly 60x longer per block
inside the RD search itself, so the same absolute saving is ~1/60 of the
relative saving it is at p10.

**What is NOT closed.** The alloc + libc-mem excess over C is still ~16 points
of the port's self time (`libsystem_malloc` ~10%, `libsystem_platform` ~9%,
against C's 0.49% / 2.70%), an arithmetic ceiling of ~1.20x that is untouched
by any of this — and the one prescribed way to take it, pooling the buffers,
measured null (`alloc_bufpool_null_2026-08-13.meta`). The remaining 3.06x at
p10 is per-pixel compute in MD/partition (24%), transforms (17%) and
entropy/coeff (16%), plus that allocator excess.
